00:04:37.660 --> 00:04:52.500
Carly Huitema (University of Guelph): There we go. Duplicity and impersonation in digital identity. When we build… when we build digital identity systems, we assume one entity controls one identity through a valid chain of cryptographic keys.

29
00:04:53.170 --> 00:05:02.949
Carly Huitema (University of Guelph): Keys may rotate over time, but the history of control should remain consistent. Two major risks threaten that assumption, impersonation and duplicity.

30
00:05:03.310 --> 00:05:04.550
Carly Huitema (University of Guelph): I don't see…

31
00:05:05.600 --> 00:05:13.560
Carly Huitema (University of Guelph): Impersonation happens when someone steals your keys. They can act as you and continue updating your identity, even though you've lost control.

32
00:05:15.960 --> 00:05:30.479
Carly Huitema (University of Guelph): Duplicity is different. It happens when the identity owner creates two conflicting versions of their own history, showing one version to one party and another version elsewhere. Both appear valid independently, but they cannot both be true.

33
00:05:31.130 --> 00:05:34.590
Carly Huitema (University of Guelph): Traditionally, we solve these problems.

34
00:05:35.060 --> 00:05:52.360
Carly Huitema (University of Guelph): Traditionally, we solve these problems using trusted third parties. A wedding certificate, for example, includes witnesses who independently confirm what happened. Later, blockchains extended this idea by acting as global… as a global witness. Everyone shares the same history.

35
00:05:56.260 --> 00:06:04.240
Carly Huitema (University of Guelph): New decentralized identity systems like DidWebVH and DidWebS use a different model based on watchers and witnesses.

36
00:06:04.960 --> 00:06:19.900
Carly Huitema (University of Guelph): Witnesses are selected by the identity owner. Whenever the identity changes, witnesses record the event and issue cryptographic receipts proving they observed it. They don't control the identity, they simply provide an independent record of history.

37
00:06:21.700 --> 00:06:31.579
Carly Huitema (University of Guelph): This helps both impersonation and duplicity. Conflicting histories or suspicious changes become visible because witnesses have recorded what they actually saw.

38
00:06:32.430 --> 00:06:45.100
Carly Huitema (University of Guelph): Watchers add another layer. They are chosen by the verifier, not the identity owner. Watchers compare an identity's history against witness records and look for inconsistencies.

39
00:06:45.420 --> 00:07:04.469
Carly Huitema (University of Guelph): Together, witnesses and watchers create decentralized accountability. Instead of relying on a central authority, trust comes from transparent records and independent verification. The goal is not to prevent every attack in advance, but to ensure that impersonation or duplicity can be detected and proven when it occurs.

