214
00:26:32.350 --> 00:26:46.849
Carly Huitema (University of Guelph): Trust spanning protocol. Trust for the way we communicate. When we communicate online, how do we know who we're actually communicating with? The internet is very good at moving messages from one place to another, but moving a message isn't the same as knowing who is on the other end.

215
00:26:46.850 --> 00:27:06.470
Carly Huitema (University of Guelph): The Trust Spanning Protocol, or TSP, is designed to provide that missing layer of trust. First, TSP authenticates the parties communicating. This helps protect against impersonation by providing a way to establish that a message really comes from the party it claims to come from, and is intended for the party receiving it.

216
00:27:06.510 --> 00:27:24.580
Carly Huitema (University of Guelph): Second, TSP is agnostic about the underlying network transport. It doesn't tie trusted communication to a particular network protocol or technology. This means trust can span different networks and transport mechanisms, helping make the protocol useful as the communication landscape evolves.

217
00:27:24.580 --> 00:27:28.339
Carly Huitema (University of Guelph): But there is another part… important part of trust, privacy.

218
00:27:28.340 --> 00:27:39.839
Carly Huitema (University of Guelph): Even if the content of a message is protected, information about the communication itself can reveal a lot. Knowing who is talking to whom and when can create a valuable trail of metadata.

219
00:27:39.930 --> 00:27:44.940
Carly Huitema (University of Guelph): TSP includes mechanisms designed to make that kind of correlation more difficult.

220
00:27:44.980 --> 00:27:56.079
Carly Huitema (University of Guelph): By using techniques such as nested messages and intermediary routing, the way a message travels can be altered so that an observer has less ability to connect the sender and recipient.

221
00:27:56.080 --> 00:28:06.590
Carly Huitema (University of Guelph): The result is a protocol designed around three important principles. Know who you're communicating with, don't depend on a particular network, and protect information about the communication itself.

222
00:28:06.590 --> 00:28:15.339
Carly Huitema (University of Guelph): That's the goal of the Trust Spanning Protocol, providing a foundation for trusted communication that can work across networks, across technologies, and into the future.

